WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.020
Film Courage: Do you get nervous when there
is nothing in the works [screenwriting wise]

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:06.589
or is there a sense of freedom because of
the possibilities of what could happen?

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:07.990
Do you almost thrive off of that?

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:08.990
Screenwriter Gary Goldstein: Good question.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.160
I don’t know if I thrive?

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:17.580
Honestly because careers can be such roller
coasters and as a writer you can go through

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:22.910
many periods where you are really not working
for hire and hopefully you are writing on

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:26.740
your own and cranking out material and utilizing
the free time.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.970
But because there can be a lot of downtime.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.190
It can be nerve wracking.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:39.640
Honestly, the ideal thing is to have enough
work along the way to keep you financially

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:43.990
solvent and have a little down time in between
to work on your own projects.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:45.850
You know based upon what you like to do.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:50.490
I mean I like writing stage plays, so I like
to have time here and there.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:56.020
Recently I had shoulder surgery and I was
home (mostly I didn’t leave the house for

00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:01.990
6 weeks) and I could only type with one hand
but I was able to use my other hand.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:06.580
And I just said “You know what, I’m going
to finish something at the end of this 6 weeks.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.190
It’s tough being here.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:09.190
I’m in pain.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:10.730
I’m going to finish writing something.”

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:15.700
And I had started a new play before I had
the surgery and I had literally just finished

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.210
this new play at the end of this period and
it was just so great.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.960
And I’m very happy with that particular
play.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.140
I’m looking forward to continuing to work
on it.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.580
But I didn’t have an assignment at that
time so it did give me the freedom to write

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.990
it and during that period not feeling all
that great, it was really nice not to have

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.210
someone waiting for something from me because
I didn’t want to have to disappoint them

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.320
because of the pain or whatever.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.610
So that was a good period to not be on assignment.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:49.340
But by and large I like it to be, air on the
side of more work, more paperwork, more assignment

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.340
work than not.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.580
It’s very, it’s like charging your batteries
are charged.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.920
I like knowing there is stuff out there happening.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:04.729
But I’m always just working on something
and writing something or trying to market

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:08.240
old scripts or work with my agent and get
new meetings and make things happen.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.569
So I really utilized the time to do that.

00:02:10.569 --> 00:02:14.670
What’s nice about having a little free time
is that you can get back to your career and

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.470
your bigger picture of your career.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:21.250
Because when you’re nose-to-the-grindstone
sometimes you forget “Well, I did have that

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.400
script I was trying to sell.”

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:25.630
Or “I still did want to get that assignment
there” Or “I want to be on that show there”

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:30.770
or whatever and you just can’t do it because
you’re too focused on your work and the

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:31.770
issue at hand.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.750
So it’s nice to have a combination of both.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:38.360
But you’re probably better off having the
work (more work than not, I think).

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:43.660
Film Courage: Well, it goes back to what you
said about focusing too much on the minutia,

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.150
so it sounds like even though you do like
to be working and you like to have stuff going,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:53.460
it sounds like to have a little bit of breathing
room, you can stand back and see more.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:59.080
Gary Goldstein: Yeah, you can sometimes lose
the big picture when you’re in the cave.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.340
And then when you get out of it then you can
say “Okay, out now.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.000
I’ve got a little time.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.380
Where am I?”

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:13.390
Because as you know, as a writer (and it’s
similar for directors and producers) you have

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:18.110
to be the master of your own ship, master
of your own destiny in terms of your career.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:22.161
You have to keep it moving forward if you
want to keep working.

00:03:22.161 --> 00:03:24.980
And an agent is great but they can do so much.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:31.080
It’s really up to us to really stay on top
of it and focused in term of what our end-game

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:32.161
is or what our goals are.

00:03:32.161 --> 00:03:34.870
What are my goals?

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:41.760
To keep writing movies that get made and write
things that are meaningful to me and that

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:47.050
I hope people will enjoy and all of that and
tell my stories and that kind of thing.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.550
But that doesn’t just happen.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:54.170
And sometimes you have to really just keep
your eye on the ball because time goes very

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.880
fast.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.460
And if it’s not you, it will be somebody
else and we have a finite amount of time on

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:01.460
this planet.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:02.460
Film Courage: Absolutely.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.510
And going back to when you left one career
(and you don’t have to go into too many

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:10.970
details about it) but were you able to step
back from a lot of minutia at that point because

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.030
you probably had more free time of your hands?

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:17.519
Gary Goldstein: It was a really weird time
because I was so used to getting a steady

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:18.519
paycheck.

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:26.129
Fortunately I had stashed away enough money
to go through this process until I got work

00:04:26.129 --> 00:04:27.129
again.

00:04:27.129 --> 00:04:32.979
But that was the thing to kind of be okay
about just getting sporadic paychecks and

00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:36.800
not knowing when the next paycheck was going
to be and then knowing that this was just

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:42.860
a safety net (basically) and that part was
difficult.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:48.129
But when I left that job and started to become
a screenwriter (TV writer at that point),

00:04:48.129 --> 00:04:51.469
it was all focus all the time for me.

00:04:51.469 --> 00:04:53.800
I mean it was like I just dove into the deep
end.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.860
And I mean there was so much to do and so
much to write.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:00.660
It was…like starting a whole new career.

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:06.719
It was just a lot to learn and be out there
a lot and really just keep my eye on the ball.

00:05:06.719 --> 00:05:12.949
So it was an exciting time but definitely
challenging.

00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:15.409
It was not a lot of down time.

00:05:15.409 --> 00:05:18.380
And also back then I would feel very guilty
about having down time.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:21.749
I would feel like I shouldn’t be going to
the movies, I should be working on my new

00:05:21.749 --> 00:05:23.020
spec script or something.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.159
And I mean that was hard.

00:05:24.159 --> 00:05:28.409
It took a long time for me to not feel guilty
for not working but that eventually happened

00:05:28.409 --> 00:05:31.969
when I was like “Okay, I’ve got enough
work and I don’t want to be working at that

00:05:31.969 --> 00:05:32.969
moment.”

00:05:32.969 --> 00:05:34.050
That was okay.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.570
But you got through phases, every career has
its phases.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:43.560
And we grow as people as you experience more
and as you become more confident or just more

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:50.284
self-possessed or just less beholden to the
forces out there.

00:05:50.284 --> 00:05:52.360
Film Courage: Well I think too, it probably
didn’t take training for you to get out

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:55.199
of that 9-to-5 or whatever it was 9-to-7 mindset?

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:59.610
Gary Goldstein: Well it kept me pretty structured.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:04.909
I was so used to that kind of 9-to-5 (well
that job was 9-to-9).

00:06:04.909 --> 00:06:11.110
But I was used to getting up early, used to
getting my work done, used to having a goal

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:12.110
for everyday.

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:14.960
So that translated really well when I was
on my own.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.439
Because otherwise I would have been like “Oh,
my gosh.

00:06:16.439 --> 00:06:17.479
How am I going to organize this?

00:06:17.479 --> 00:06:18.479
What am I going to do?”

00:06:18.479 --> 00:06:19.479
That part wasn’t an issue.

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:21.770
It was just like “Will I get a job?

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:22.770
Will I be successful?

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:26.849
Will I really be able to make a go of this?”

